WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:02.860
He's the artist formerly known as McLovin.

00:00:02.860 --> 00:00:07.270
And after his breakout performance in Superbad,
Christopher Mintz-Plasse seemed like he was

00:00:07.270 --> 00:00:12.480
on the verge of joining co-stars Jonah Hill
and Seth Rogen on Hollywood's A-list, following

00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:16.200
it up with acclaimed turns in Kick-Ass and
How to Train Your Dragon.

00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:20.699
But his popularity with fans somehow didn't
translate into more big time roles.

00:00:20.699 --> 00:00:25.310
Here's a look at why Hollywood won't cast
Christopher Mintz-Plasse anymore.

00:00:25.310 --> 00:00:27.460
The curse of McLovin

00:00:27.460 --> 00:00:32.951
Ever since 2007's Superbad, where he obtained
the world's fakest fake ID, Christopher Mintz-Plasse

00:00:32.951 --> 00:00:35.390
has been better known by another name:

00:00:35.390 --> 00:00:36.390
"McLovin?"

00:00:36.390 --> 00:00:37.390
"Yeah."

00:00:37.390 --> 00:00:38.390
"McLovin?

00:00:40.300 --> 00:00:41.160
What kind of stupid name is that, Fogel?

00:00:41.160 --> 00:00:43.190
What, are you trying to be an Irish R&amp;B singer?"

00:00:43.190 --> 00:00:47.460
Nearly ten years later, it's still by far
his most memorable role — which is fine

00:00:47.460 --> 00:00:51.970
if you want to be a cult figure forever, but
not so great for the establishment of a robust

00:00:51.970 --> 00:00:54.170
and continuing career in Hollywood.

00:00:54.170 --> 00:00:59.350
The legacy of McLovin has utterly overshadowed
literally every movie Mintz-Plasse has made

00:00:59.350 --> 00:01:00.350
since.

00:01:00.350 --> 00:01:04.600
It's a fair bet that at least half his fans
don't even recognize him by his real name.

00:01:04.600 --> 00:01:08.090
Turns out that fake ID wasn't so fake after
all.

00:01:08.090 --> 00:01:10.390
He's focused on his music

00:01:10.390 --> 00:01:14.600
While Christopher Mintz-Plasse definitely
enjoys acting, he's also a dedicated musician,

00:01:14.600 --> 00:01:19.219
and his projects on that front are clearly
just as important to him as being on-screen.

00:01:19.219 --> 00:01:23.900
He's spending a lot of time these days practicing,
performing, and recording with his latest

00:01:23.900 --> 00:01:28.249
band, Mainman — which is, of course, time
he's not spending auditioning for more or

00:01:28.249 --> 00:01:30.930
bigger roles in blockbuster Hollywood movies.

00:01:30.930 --> 00:01:35.740
Which is just how he likes it, telling Stereogum,
"I would love to keep balancing the two.

00:01:35.740 --> 00:01:39.969
I think I'm very lucky and grateful for where
I am right now, that I can do both.

00:01:39.969 --> 00:01:43.409
Acting, you can work for three months and
not work for six months.

00:01:43.409 --> 00:01:47.319
So in that time, I love the momentum of music
and everything that's going on.

00:01:47.319 --> 00:01:49.250
The momentum right now is very musical.

00:01:49.250 --> 00:01:52.709
I kinda just go with the flow."

00:01:52.709 --> 00:01:54.509
Doesn't court celebrity

00:01:54.509 --> 00:02:00.020
While his Superbad castmates seem to be constantly
in our faces at every possibly opportunity,

00:02:00.020 --> 00:02:03.999
Mintz-Plasse has kept a much lower profile,
with a media presence that leans more towards

00:02:03.999 --> 00:02:07.399
photos of him cuddling with dogs than mugging
for the cameras.

00:02:07.399 --> 00:02:12.060
While it may keep his name out of the tabloids,
keeping a low profile in Hollywood isn't exactly

00:02:12.060 --> 00:02:13.420
the best career move.

00:02:13.420 --> 00:02:17.530
When it comes to making movies, out of sight
is definitely out of mind.

00:02:17.530 --> 00:02:19.470
Heard but not seen

00:02:19.470 --> 00:02:23.500
Christopher Mintz-Plasse has been a go-to
guy for Dreamworks' animation studio ever

00:02:23.500 --> 00:02:27.070
since he scored the role of Fishlegs in How
to Train Your Dragon.

00:02:27.070 --> 00:02:31.100
In addition to voicing that character through
three films and a TV series, the actor was

00:02:31.100 --> 00:02:36.110
also the first call when they needed a villain's
voice for the 2016 Trolls movie.

00:02:36.110 --> 00:02:40.870
Sure, it's nice work, but being a voiceover
actor is just another way to be pigeonholed,

00:02:40.870 --> 00:02:43.840
something Mintz-Plasse has already had far
too much of.

00:02:43.840 --> 00:02:48.460
"The only way I'll ever be happy is by eating
a troll, and that ain't gonna happen thanks

00:02:48.460 --> 00:02:49.460
to you!"

00:02:50.030 --> 00:02:51.500
Trapped in a sitcom

00:02:51.500 --> 00:02:56.720
In May 2012, Mintz-Plasse scored the lead
role in a CBS sitcom called Friend Me.

00:02:56.720 --> 00:03:00.960
It seemed like the perfect vehicle to transition
from movies to television, but tragically,

00:03:00.960 --> 00:03:05.310
Friend Me creator Alan Kirschenbaum took his
own life before the project premiered, and

00:03:05.310 --> 00:03:10.070
CBS decided not only to leave the show out
of their lineup, but not to even air a single

00:03:10.070 --> 00:03:12.510
one of the eight episodes already in the can.

00:03:12.510 --> 00:03:16.290
The double blow of losing both Kirschenbaum
and the show was hard for Mintz-Plasse to

00:03:16.290 --> 00:03:17.440
bounce back from.

00:03:17.440 --> 00:03:22.790
But in 2016, he finally landed another leading
sitcom role, alongside Joel McHale and Stephen

00:03:22.790 --> 00:03:26.310
Fry on the CBS sitcom The Great Indoors.

00:03:26.310 --> 00:03:30.220
Unfortunately, that show has been panned by
critics, and hasn't exactly set the ratings

00:03:30.220 --> 00:03:31.340
on fire either.

00:03:31.340 --> 00:03:35.980
Rotten Tomatoes described the show as "one
repetitive, formulaic joke," which hardly

00:03:35.980 --> 00:03:40.370
seems to suit an actor like Mintz-Plasse,
who thrives playing against type in edgier,

00:03:40.370 --> 00:03:41.640
boundary pushing projects.

00:03:41.640 --> 00:03:45.920
Hopefully the show gets a lot better before
Mintz-Plasse gets stuck in the downward spiral

00:03:45.920 --> 00:03:47.720
of lowbrow sitcoms.

00:03:47.720 --> 00:03:49.680
"Check burn!"

00:03:49.680 --> 00:03:51.500
He can turn it around

00:03:51.500 --> 00:03:55.880
Part of the reason for Christopher Mintz-Plasse's
low visibility in Hollywood is that he seems

00:03:55.880 --> 00:03:57.071
to prefer things that way.

00:03:57.071 --> 00:04:02.060
But he still has both the chops and the connections
in the industry to make a comeback if he wants

00:04:02.060 --> 00:04:03.060
to.

00:04:03.060 --> 00:04:04.060
"Hoo-ah!"

00:04:05.620 --> 00:04:06.620
"That's Al Pacino!"

00:04:06.620 --> 00:04:07.140
"That's not even -- yeah."

00:04:07.150 --> 00:04:11.510
So if he ever decides to commit completely
to acting, and to pursuing more screen time

00:04:11.510 --> 00:04:15.901
in bigger movies, becoming a star will probably
be as simple as making a few phone calls to

00:04:15.901 --> 00:04:17.310
Rogen and the Apatow crew.

00:04:18.260 --> 00:04:19.520
"You wanted me to be my own man.

00:04:19.530 --> 00:04:21.750
This is what it looks like."

00:04:23.260 --> 00:04:24.360
"Alright, alright, I'll make some calls."

00:04:24.840 --> 00:04:25.840
"Yes!"

00:04:25.840 --> 00:04:26.840
Fingers crossed!

00:04:26.840 --> 00:04:27.840
Thanks for watching!

00:04:27.840 --> 00:04:30.460
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:04:30.460 --> 00:04:33.010
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

